{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Sea Otter's Graveyard: Chitin Rush on a Kelp Urchin Barren",
    "date": "2026-11-15",
    "lesson": "When sea otters disappear from a kelp forest, purple sea urchins explode in number and mow down the kelp canopy, leaving a bare 'urchin barren'. But urchins also die in mass die-offs (disease, starvation after they exhaust the kelp), and each urchin test (shell) is loaded with chitin in its tube-feet and in the mouthparts of the decomposing crustacean fauna that used to live in the canopy. This scenario models the bacterial feast that follows an urchin die-off on a barren: chitinolytic copiotrophs (Vibrio- and Pseudoalteromonas-like) bloom fast on the falling chitin debris, racing grazing protists and a trailing phage bloom that eventually crashes the party - a smaller, shallower, colder echo of a whale-fall or squid-fall bonanza, but driven by a trophic cascade instead of a single carcass.",
    "citation": "Estes & Palmisano 1974; Konar & Estes 2003 (urchin barrens); chitin-degrading Vibrio succession studies",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Sea otter decline -> purple urchin (Strongylocentrotus purpuratus) barren formation and die-off chitin pulses, central California kelp forest, ~15-20m depth"
  },
